import{_ as a,o as t,c as n,a2 as o}from"./chunks/framework.DBYUAoKZ.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"The admin area","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"docs/admin.md","filePath":"docs/admin.md"}'),s={name:"docs/admin.md"};function i(r,e,d,c,h,l){return t(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="the-admin-area" tabindex="-1">The admin area <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-admin-area" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The admin area&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The admin area is where the maintainer looks after the catalog, the accounts and the server&#39;s settings. It is a section of the website under <code>/admin</code> (<a href="https://github.com/tkint/coach-poulpy/issues/26" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#26</a>). It never shows workouts or health data.</p><p>Every route it uses is under <code>/api/v1/admin/</code> and described in the OpenAPI document, which is the full reference. This page says what each screen does and why.</p><h2 id="who-can-open-it" tabindex="-1">Who can open it <a class="header-anchor" href="#who-can-open-it" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Who can open it&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Admin is a role on the account (<code>member</code> or <code>admin</code>), not a scope anyone can ask for (<a href="./adr/0020-every-credential-resolves-to-one-caller">ADR 0020</a>). There is one admin for now and no finer rights.</p><p>Only the website&#39;s sign-in reaches it, with cookies (<a href="./the-api#browser-session-admin-website">the API</a>). Everyone else is turned away:</p><ul><li>An anonymous caller gets 401.</li><li>A member, an admin on the phone app, a personal access token or a third-party app gets 403.</li><li>A write whose <code>Origin</code> is not the website&#39;s gets 403. This is the CSRF defence of ADR 0020.</li></ul><p>A member who tries to sign in on the website is signed out at once with the usual &quot;wrong email or password&quot;, so the sign-in never reveals an account&#39;s role.</p><h2 id="making-the-first-admin" tabindex="-1">Making the first admin <a class="header-anchor" href="#making-the-first-admin" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Making the first admin&quot;">​</a></h2><p>No endpoint makes an admin. Set <code>ADMIN_EMAIL</code> in the <code>production</code> environment (<a href="./releasing#deploy-the-server">Releasing</a>) and deploy. When the server starts, the confirmed account with that address becomes an admin. An address with no confirmed account does nothing, so signing up first with someone else&#39;s address can&#39;t take the role.</p><h2 id="accounts" tabindex="-1">Accounts <a class="header-anchor" href="#accounts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Accounts&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <strong>Accounts</strong> screen lists accounts with email, role, confirmation, sign-in status and creation date. You can filter, sort, page and open an account by id. The view lives in the address, so it can be bookmarked (<a href="./web-app#navigation-lives-in-the-url">navigation</a>).</p><p>An admin can:</p><ul><li><strong>Disable</strong> an account. It can&#39;t sign in and every device is signed out. <strong>Enable</strong> reverses it. A disabled account gets the same &quot;wrong email or password&quot; as any other refusal.</li><li><strong>Delete</strong> an account after typing its email again. It removes the account and all its data, through the same code as a user deleting their own account.</li></ul><p>An admin can&#39;t disable or delete their own account.</p><p>Filtering and paging happen on the server by reading every account row and cutting the page in memory. That is enough for a back office.</p><h2 id="settings" tabindex="-1">Settings <a class="header-anchor" href="#settings" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Settings&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <strong>Settings</strong> screen changes the email from-address without a deploy.</p><ul><li><strong>Only non-secret settings live in the database.</strong> The SMTP host, port, user and password stay in the environment (<a href="./deploying-the-server">Deploying the server</a>) and never show here.</li><li><strong><code>MAIL_FROM</code> is the default</strong> until an address is saved. A saved address applies from the next email, with no restart. Both <code>hello@example.com</code> and <code>Coach Poulpy &lt;hello@example.com&gt;</code> work. Anything else is refused and nothing is saved.</li><li><strong>Send test email</strong> sends a message to the signed-in admin and says whether the mail server accepted it.</li></ul><h2 id="status" tabindex="-1">Status <a class="header-anchor" href="#status" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Status&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <strong>Status</strong> screen reports the server&#39;s mail setup and who is waiting to confirm. It is read-only, apart from one action (<a href="https://github.com/tkint/coach-poulpy/issues/340" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#340</a>).</p><ul><li><strong>Mail</strong> is configured when <code>SMTP_HOST</code> is set. Without it, sending fails with <code>No mail server is set: SMTP_HOST is unset</code>. Otherwise the screen shows the last email since the server started: its kind (confirmation, password reset or test), when, and whether it worked. A restart forgets it. The SMTP details are never shown.</li><li><strong>Pending registrations</strong> are accounts whose email isn&#39;t confirmed, newest first, the last 100. Each row shows the address, when it signed up and when its latest link expires.</li><li><strong>Confirm by hand</strong> (<a href="https://github.com/tkint/coach-poulpy/issues/382" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#382</a>) lets an admin confirm a pending row, for example when the email never arrived or on a laptop with no mail server (<a href="./the-api#trying-it-locally">Trying it locally</a>). It has the same effect as following the link. A dialog first says the admin vouches for an address nobody proved.</li></ul><p>The public <code>/status</code> page of the web app is separate. Anyone can open it. It says only whether Coach Poulpy is up or down, never how the server is configured, and a test holds it to that.</p><h2 id="catalog" tabindex="-1">Catalog <a class="header-anchor" href="#catalog" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Catalog&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The server keeps the exercise catalog in two forms: a <strong>catalog draft</strong> the admin edits, and <strong>published revisions</strong> that phones download. Each exercise has its own numbered revisions (1, 2, 3), so publishing writes only the exercises it names (<a href="./adr/0030-the-catalog-changes-by-exercise-revision-not-by-catalog-version">ADR 0030</a>). The first start seeds both from the catalog the server was built with.</p><p>The admin has an exercise list at <code>/admin/catalog</code> and an editor at <code>/admin/catalog/&lt;slug&gt;</code>. Publishing and retiring are in the list&#39;s selection menu (<a href="https://github.com/tkint/coach-poulpy/issues/338" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#338</a>).</p><ul><li><strong>An exercise is the catalog&#39;s JSON</strong>, with every text a map from language to string. Saving needs the English name. Other languages can wait, but publishing needs English and French.</li><li><strong>A slug can change only until the exercise is first published.</strong> Workouts point at it, so after that a rename is refused (<a href="./adr/0008-curated-catalog-with-stable-slugs">ADR 0008</a>).</li><li><strong>Exercises are retired, never deleted.</strong> A retired exercise can be offered again.</li><li><strong>Publishing</strong> gives each named exercise its next revision, or every exercise with a change when none is named. It is refused when a translation is missing or when nothing has changed. Phones see the new revisions at once.</li></ul><details><summary>Filters, change states and responses</summary><p>The list filters by <code>q</code> (slug, a name in any language or an alias), <code>pattern</code>, <code>muscles</code>, <code>positions</code>, <code>equipment</code>, <code>translation</code>, <code>content</code> and <code>lifecycle</code>, and sorts by <code>name</code>, <code>pattern</code>, <code>languages</code>, <code>change</code> and <code>status</code>. The OpenAPI document has the exact values.</p><p>An exercise&#39;s <strong>change</strong> against its latest published revision is <code>added</code>, <code>edited</code>, <code>retired</code>, <code>offered</code> or none. Its <strong>lifecycle</strong> is <code>active</code>, <code>retiring</code> (retired in the draft, not yet in a published revision) or <code>retired</code>. <code>GET /api/v1/admin/catalog/changes</code> previews what publishing would do, with each edited field&#39;s before and after.</p><p>Refusals: <code>409 slug-published</code>, <code>409 slug-taken</code>, <code>409 missing-translations</code> (listing exercises and languages), <code>409 no-changes</code>, <code>400 invalid-exercise</code> and <code>400 bad-query</code>.</p></details><h2 id="the-audit-log" tabindex="-1">The audit log <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-audit-log" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The audit log&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every admin write is recorded in an audit log: who did it, what, and the target. The first screen of the admin shows the last 100 entries. Entries stay when the admin&#39;s own account is deleted, and one with no author means the server did it, as at start-up.</p><p>Targets never hold an address in clear. An account is recorded by its id, an email by its masked form (<code>a***@example.com</code>), and a password never appears. The <code>EmailAddress</code> type prints masked, so an address that reaches a log by accident stays hidden.</p><details><summary>The actions that are recorded</summary><p><code>admin.account.disable</code>, <code>admin.account.enable</code>, <code>admin.account.delete</code>, <code>admin.account.confirm</code>, <code>admin.settings.mail-from</code>, <code>admin.catalog.create</code>, <code>admin.catalog.edit</code>, <code>admin.catalog.retire</code>, <code>admin.catalog.offer</code> and <code>admin.catalog.publish</code>, plus the promotion of the first admin.</p><p>In the code, each is an <code>AdminAction</code> event sent through http4k&#39;s <code>Events</code>. One sink stores it in <code>admin_audit</code> and another prints it to the server log. Tests check it with <code>RecordingEvents</code>.</p></details>',32)])])}const m=a(s,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
